WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.336
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.336 --> 00:00:09.070
Think back to the last time
you were in a hospital.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.090
What do you see?

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.340
Maybe you walked through clear glass
doors into a gleaming white lobby.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:23.230
You see doctors in white coats,
stethoscopes draped around their necks.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:27.360
You see nurses in blue scrubs,
something a little bit like this.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.580
Maybe you haven't actually been in
a hospital in a little while, but

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.740
you've watched a lot of Grey's Anatomy and
House.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.180
And so, you can just picture
those same doctors and

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.660
nurses floating off in corners of
the hospital solving medical mysteries.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.900
Well, I've spent the last five years
working in and with hospitals.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:46.820
And that's not what I see.

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:53.990
In fact, every time I would walk into
a new hospital, I would go to the door and

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:59.940
put my hand out for the handle and
I would hesitate before I opened.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.120
Do I really want to go inside?

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:10.024
Hospitals have a problem, and
this problem is killing us.

00:01:10.024 --> 00:01:15.270
99,000, 99,000 people

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:19.278
every year are killed by this
problem in the United States.

00:01:19.278 --> 00:01:24.473
1 in 20,
1 in 20 patients who steps foot into

00:01:24.473 --> 00:01:29.308
a hospital would be
affected by this problem.

00:01:29.308 --> 00:01:31.368
What is this problem?

00:01:31.368 --> 00:01:32.545
Do you know?

00:01:32.545 --> 00:01:37.327
It's not common knowledge, so
there is a good chance that you don't.

00:01:37.327 --> 00:01:40.775
Hospital-acquired infections.

00:01:40.775 --> 00:01:44.240
Hospital-acquired infections
are infections that a person did not have

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.990
prior to entering the hospital in which
they would not have otherwise contracted

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.040
had they not been in hospital.

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:53.010
They're almost entirely preventable.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:58.740
And yet, in 2020, they will kill
99,000 people in the United States.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.710
That's 1 person every 5 minutes.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.370
That's two times the number
of people who would be killed

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:05.790
in car accidents this year.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:09.510
That's three times the number of
people who will be killed by guns

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:12.100
in the United States in 2020.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.463
How can this happen?

00:02:17.032 --> 00:02:20.011
Well, there is a system that
is broken in the hospital and

00:02:20.011 --> 00:02:21.759
I'm going to show you what that is.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.840
So let's think again
about our hospital here.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.590
We walk inside, and we look around, and
there is something that we cannot see.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.910
And it's everywhere in the hospital.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:33.540
Germs.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.960
But these aren't you normal germs, these
are not the germs that you'll find down at

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.740
Starbucks or
at your local office building.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.741
These germs are what are called superbugs.

00:02:46.537 --> 00:02:49.065
These superbugs go by many names.

00:02:49.065 --> 00:02:52.756
MRSA, Clostridium difficile or C diff,

00:02:52.756 --> 00:02:57.740
Candida auris, CRE, VRE,
and they are deadly.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:58.740
To put this in perspective for

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:03.030
you, everybody now knows about
this 2019 novel Coronavirus.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.510
Well, if you catch the Coronavirus,
you have a 2% chance of dying.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:11.130
If you catch MRSA or
C diff or Candida auris or

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:15.100
one of these other super bugs,
you have up to a 30% chance of dying.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.460
These superbugs are also widespread.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.280
So, as of today, March 3rd, 2020,

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:25.700
approximately 90,000 people have
been infected with the Coronavirus.

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:28.240
And that's worldwide.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.230
But just this year and just in the United
States, 1.7 million people will be

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:37.187
infected with one of these superbugs that
leads to hospital-acquired infection.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.430
So you don't need to get into a plane and
fly off to Wuhan.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:45.090
Or you don't need to get into a cruise
ship that's stuck off the coast of Japan

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.730
to be exposed to some the world's
most deadliest germs.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:50.940
All you have to do is get into a car and
drive down to your local hospital.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.460
So let's come back again
to our hospital here.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.280
It's a beautiful hospital.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.040
And we walk inside.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.980
There's something that we don't
see when we're in this hospital.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:10.020
What we don't see is the people who are
dedicated to cleaning up the superbugs.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.050
I'm going to call these
people our superheroes.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.064
And who are these superheroes?

00:04:15.064 --> 00:04:19.349
Are they our doctors or our nurses or
hospital administrators?

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.190
No, our superheroes
are the environmental services staff.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:28.410
They are the janitors and
the housekeepers of the hospital.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:33.400
So I'm going to tell you a story
about one of these superheroes.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:34.180
Her name is Marie.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.120
So Marie is a mother of four children.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.477
She works three jobs,

00:04:39.477 --> 00:04:43.130
part time jobs at minimum wage
just to barely make ends meet.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:47.690
She tells herself she can keep it
all together but she's exhausted.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.290
She's probably slept on average
about four hours every night for

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:53.160
the past, God knows how many weeks.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:56.239
But she's not just exhausted
from the lack of sleep.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:01.600
She's exhausted from the heroic
job that she does in the hospital.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.088
So let's take a minute to consider
everything that Marie needs to do,

00:05:05.088 --> 00:05:08.350
every time she walks into a new
hospital patient room to clean it.

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:10.670
And she has to do this over and
over again throughout the day.

00:05:12.482 --> 00:05:16.200
So Marie walks into this hospital room,
she has to strip the linens from the bed.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.890
She then has to inspect the mattress,
make sure that there are no punctures or

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.420
holes or other issues of the mattress,
that's very important.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.465
She then has to remake
the bed with fresh linens.

00:05:25.465 --> 00:05:30.095
She then has to disinfect every single
surface within that hospital room.

00:05:30.095 --> 00:05:31.525
So everything from the light switches,

00:05:31.525 --> 00:05:35.815
the doorknobs, the bedside table,
the overhead table, the bed rails.

00:05:35.815 --> 00:05:40.165
She has to disinfect pull cords, all of
the medical equipment, any chairs and

00:05:40.165 --> 00:05:41.585
their arm rails.

00:05:41.585 --> 00:05:46.130
She then has to go into the bathroom, and
she has to disinfect the sink, the faucet,

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:50.390
the drain, the toilet,
the guard rails on the side of the toilet.

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:55.021
She then has to disinfect the pool
cords in there and then disinfect and

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:58.567
mop the floor in the patient room and
in the bathroom.

00:05:58.567 --> 00:06:02.180
How much time do you think we should
give superhero, Marie, to do this job?

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:06.000
I'll give you a hint.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.819
So in the hotel industry,

00:06:07.819 --> 00:06:13.452
housekeepers are given on average 30
minutes to turn a room in between guests.

00:06:17.447 --> 00:06:20.360
But hotels are not
a critical care environment.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:23.184
Hotels don't have patience.

00:06:26.242 --> 00:06:31.507
In hospitals, Marie is only given 10
minutes, just 10 minutes to change

00:06:31.507 --> 00:06:36.370
this patient room and do all those
critical things that need to be done.

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:37.900
10 minutes.

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:42.830
Just imagine the next time you go into
a hotel, do you think that the housekeeper

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:47.540
only took 10 minutes in between cleaning
the room between you and the prior guest?

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:52.650
Now, imagine that that prior guest
had a deadly colon infection.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.260
I mean, those rooms are not clean.

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:55.200
It's really disgusting.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.360
So, there's something else that
you don't see about Marie, and

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:03.079
that is that she is not respected in
this hospital, and neither is her job.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:06.510
Just yesterday she was
yelled at by a nurse for

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:09.810
not turning an operating room
quickly enough, even though she knew

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:13.140
that moving any faster would
seriously compromise patient safety.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:19.970
Marie also has not been given
the right tools for her job.

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:23.710
She's been given disinfectants that are
ineffective, they're the cheapest possible

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:26.960
disinfectants and they don't work against
the superbugs that we're concerned about.

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:32.120
She's given mops and wipes that come
back from the laundry contaminated.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:34.770
She just reintroduces whatever
she took out yesterday

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:36.000
back into the hospital environment.

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:39.050
Marie was also never trained properly.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:42.570
The prevalent feeling among
hospital administrators is,

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:45.105
why would we invest in training people for
this role?

00:07:45.105 --> 00:07:47.921
It's easy, anybody can do it,
its not important.

00:07:50.845 --> 00:07:52.214
All of these things.

00:07:52.214 --> 00:07:57.265
The lack of a proper paycheck, the lack of
respect, the lack of tools and the lack

00:07:57.265 --> 00:08:02.150
of training and the lack of enough
staff to actually do this job right.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:06.320
Has contributed to an environment
where Murray just hates this job.

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:07.711
How could she not, how can you blame her?

00:08:10.057 --> 00:08:13.305
All of these factors are within
the hospital's control and yet

00:08:13.305 --> 00:08:15.680
nothing is done about this.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:19.160
And in fact, the very first
place that most hospitals look

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:22.530
when they want to trim their budget, is
at the environmental services department.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:25.760
So where does this leave us?

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:30.150
Okay, on the one hand, we have our
superbugs that are extremely deadly,

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:32.940
on the other hand,
we have our superheroes.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:36.180
And we've created a situation
where we have tied these

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:38.370
hands of our superheroes
behind their backs.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:42.580
We have made it impossible for
them to do their job.

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:45.914
It's no wonder that the superbugs
are winning in this battle versus

00:08:45.914 --> 00:08:46.856
the superheroes.

00:08:50.157 --> 00:08:52.420
All of this right now is abstract.

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:57.140
This isn't real, but
the consequences of this are real.

00:08:58.280 --> 00:08:59.857
So let me show you what you can see.

00:09:03.324 --> 00:09:06.390
This is Emma Grace Bru.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:12.980
Emma contracted a MRSA infection when she
was a newborn at a hospital in Louisiana.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:19.200
She had a twin brother, her twin
brother also contracted an infection.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.840
He died when he was 14 days old.

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:27.060
Emma lived on, she had permanent damage
to her legs, her lungs and her heart.

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:33.650
She was in and out of the hospital for
the first few years of her life.

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:35.800
When she was three and
a half she went back into the hospital for

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:38.210
yet another surgery to her leg.

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:41.070
This time when she was in the hospital,
she contracted pneumonia.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.280
The doctors told her parents that because
the MRSA had so devastated her immune

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:51.274
system when she was a newborn,
there was probably nothing they could do.

00:09:51.274 --> 00:09:55.470
And in fact, Emma die just days
shy of her fourth birthday.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:10:02.268
And this is what happens
every five minutes.

00:10:06.469 --> 00:10:08.450
But there is hope.

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:12.650
There is something that we can do.

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:14.140
So for the past five years,

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:17.500
I've been working on a company that
addresses some of these issues.

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:18.590
And I know what you're thinking.

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.260
We're sitting here in Silicon Valley,

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:22.600
there must be some sort of high
tech solution to this problem.

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:27.144
But what I love about the solution
is that it can be low tech.

00:10:29.431 --> 00:10:31.864
So my company,
we developed a platform of tools and

00:10:31.864 --> 00:10:35.541
training that we could actually plug into
a hospital to help address those two

00:10:35.541 --> 00:10:38.340
issues out of the five
that I mentioned earlier.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:42.605
And just by addressing those two issues,
we're able to reduce infections by over

00:10:42.605 --> 00:10:44.379
30% in all the hospitals
that we worked with.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:46.910
But I'm not here to talk about my company.

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:51.210
I'm here to talk about the three things
that I think all of us in this room

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:53.720
can do to actually make a difference and
help solve this problem.

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:56.670
So what are those three things?

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:58.520
The first thing is awareness.

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:00.000
That's what we're doing here right now.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:02.960
We need to talk about this issue.

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:05.210
We need to shine a light on it.

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:08.430
Hospitals have a vested interest
in keeping this issue in the dark.

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:11.960
And it's up to all of us to bring it out
of the shadows into the light of day.

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:14.780
The second thing we need to do.

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:19.590
We need to get hospitals to change how
they deal with their environmental

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:20.970
services department.

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:25.200
We need them to fix these issues and
take them seriously and

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.330
then we need to hold them accountable for
doing that.

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:29.020
So how can you hold
a hospital accountable?

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:31.250
There are now great resources online.

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:32.950
Hospital Compare is one of the best.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:36.540
It's put together by the government, and
you can actually go onto this website and

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:41.130
you can compare your local
hospital versus regional and

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:44.339
national benchmarks and see how it
stacks up in terms of infection rates.

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:50.570
We all have to remember that we are
consumers of healthcare as patients and

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:51.810
as the loved ones of patients.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:57.990
We need to send a message to hospitals,
that as customers, we can take

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:01.520
our business elsewhere if they don't
shape up and fix this infection issue.

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:05.530
And the third thing we need
to do comes down to respect.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:09.880
So, I mentioned that my company
has a training component to it.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:12.320
One of our best trainers who works with
the environmental services staff in

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:16.670
the hospital told me a story that
totally changed my perspective on this.

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:20.290
And so, he told me that when
he would go into the hospital,

00:12:20.290 --> 00:12:21.160
he was having a lot of trouble.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:22.870
He felt like he wasn't getting
through to these environmental

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:25.020
services staff to the janitors and
the housekeepers.

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:29.440
And he did one simple thing at the
beginning of each training session that

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:31.720
totally changed the mindset
in that room and

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:35.989
shifted how he interacted with
the housekeepers in the training sessions.

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:43.910
He started each session by saying, purpose
over paycheck, purpose over paycheck.

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:46.850
And just those three things
totally changed the dynamic.

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:49.335
And I think what he did was
he was showing respect.

00:12:49.335 --> 00:12:52.425
He was showing respect for
the environmental services workers.

00:12:52.425 --> 00:12:56.525
And showing them that they have a purpose
in this job and it is important.

00:12:56.525 --> 00:13:00.515
And so, I think the next time all of
you are in the hospital, you should say

00:13:00.515 --> 00:13:04.635
thank you when you see the janitor or
the housekeeper in that hospital.

00:13:04.635 --> 00:13:08.125
Show them the respect they deserve and
show them that you

00:13:08.125 --> 00:13:11.846
understand the incredibly important role
that they have in keeping patients safe.

00:13:11.846 --> 00:13:14.144
Thank you.

00:13:14.144 --> 00:13:15.388
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:13:15.388 --> 00:13:20.159
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC].

